body:
When the fighting is over you feel it before you understand it: someone standing at your shoulder who was not there, looking out over the field, counting. Dace. He counts twice. Then he is gone.

That night a Sending comes with the clink of a glass in it. 'He's back at the bar,' says Ondria. 'He won't tell me how it went. He says to ask you. So. How did it go?'

{shadow}

The bargain is struck, and as the other party rises you smell, for a moment, bread and cold stone: an almonry in a famine year. Sister Ivo is at your elbow, reading the terms over your shoulder, her bowl the right way up at her throat, the way the Fast leaves its dead. Then she is gone.

Ondria's Sending finds you that night. 'Ivo's back,' she says. 'I've filled her bowl. She wants to know what you think you did.'

{shadow}

The road asks its question and you answer it, and as you walk on you hear, very close, the creak of a drover's boots and a man humming the same four notes over and over, the way men hum when they are a day from home. Old Pell. He walks with you to the next rise. Then he is gone.

'He's back,' says Ondria's Sending, that night. 'He's stopped humming. He says you answered it. He wants to know what you'd have said if it had been him asking.'

{shadow}
